      "id": "def-classical-algebraic-prevariety-regular-maps-and-varieties",
      "location": "Definition",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The definition says that a classical algebraic prevariety is a locally ringed space merely “covered by open subspaces isomorphic ... to affine algebraic sets,” omitting quasi-compactness (equivalently, a finite affine cover). Its cited Milne Definition 5.2 requires quasi-compactness. Without it, the definition admits non-quasi-compact locally finite-type separated irreducible schemes: start with A^2, iteratively blow up a chosen point and choose a point on the new exceptional divisor, then take the directed union of the open schemes S_i minus the chosen point. This is integral, separated, locally of finite type over k, and covered by affine algebraic charts, but is not quasi-compact.",
      "severity": "fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-classical-varieties-equivalent-integral-separated-finite-type-schemes",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "overstrong-title-or-statement",
      "evidence": "The statement asserts an equivalence from “irreducible classical k-varieties” to integral finite-type k-schemes. Under the current definition of classical prevariety, the non-quasi-compact integral separated directed-union example above is an irreducible classical variety, since it has an affine-algebraic-chart cover, but it is not finite type over k. Thus it is an object of the stated source category but not of the target category, so the claimed equivalence is false. The proof’s step 3.1 simply asserts preservation of finite type and does not supply the missing quasi-compactness.",
      "severity": "fatal"
    }
